Z||000000||fierogt88||08-31-2001||02:23 PM||||The battery in my fiero died and would not hold a charge, so I tried to change it.  The bolt that holds down the battery was extremely tight, so I yanked with all my strength and it yelled loudly.  At first I thought it had broke loose, but after five minutes of ratchet action it was obvious that this was not the case.  I reached underneath the battery tray and felt a square bracket that had broken off the frame.<P>I was able to pry the old battery out and hook up the new battery, but it's not held down by anything....<P>Anybody else have this problem?<BR>What did you do to fix it?<P>TIA<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||JetroGT||08-31-2001||04:15 PM||||Mine dosen't move either. One of these days I'll get to it with a breaker bar. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Kasdan||08-31-2001||04:23 PM||||I dont even have a battery hold down, mine just kinda sits there....mocking me...<P>------------------<BR>Tyler Morgan<BR>1986 GT Fastback, <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FIERO2.JPG">       <BR>Holley Ram-Air, Power Steering, K&N Filtercharger, Bosch Wires, Bosch Platinum Plugs, Conrad Distribuiter Cap/Rotor, New GT Seats, New Headliner, New Carpet, Ocelet Exhaust, Red Underbody Light Kit (coming soon)<BR>Aim=FieroDriver1986||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||08-31-2001||07:52 PM||||If you want the OE hold down fixed....<P>The only thing I can think of is to fit it with a clip nut. You'll likely need a diferant bolt since most of the clip nuts available in that size are course threads that I've seen.<P>Alternatly you could permanently anchor an eye bolt there with a couple washers on it. Then use a rubber bunggy to hold it down.<P>I'd want it held down with something. Even small amounts of movement can wear a hole in it if it gets against something.||||reg||1
